WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:04.960
This briefing is Book 12 of the
Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.337
Security Operations and Telemetry.

4
00:00:07.337 --> 00:00:11.818
It's about how an agency makes every
part of its estate emit evidence,

5
00:00:11.818 --> 00:00:16.938
turns that evidence into detection and
response, and then proves it all works.

6
00:00:16.938 --> 00:00:18.313
We'll take four questions.

7
00:00:18.313 --> 00:00:20.475
Why can't you defend what you can't see?

8
00:00:20.475 --> 00:00:24.425
What must each plane emit, and
how do findings reach one queue?

9
00:00:24.425 --> 00:00:26.487
Who runs security operations?

10
00:00:26.487 --> 00:00:29.638
And how do you run detection,
response and monitoring?

11
00:00:29.638 --> 00:00:33.493
The short version: design
visibility instead of assuming it,

12
00:00:33.493 --> 00:00:37.812
and close a finding only when the
thing is actually fixed, with proof.

13
00:00:37.812 --> 00:00:42.887
The slides and notes carry every detail
and source; I'll stick to what matters.

14
00:00:42.887 --> 00:00:46.090
Let's start with why
visibility can't be assumed.

15
00:00:46.840 --> 00:00:49.660
Most agencies run a SIEM,
pay for log storage,

16
00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:52.803
and can show an auditor
millions of events a day.

17
00:00:52.803 --> 00:00:55.503
But every one of those
numbers measures what arrived.

18
00:00:55.503 --> 00:00:57.103
None of them measures what didn't.

19
00:00:57.103 --> 00:01:00.439
NIST keeps apart two things
that are easy to buy as one.

20
00:01:00.439 --> 00:01:03.190
One control says generate
the audit records.

21
00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:06.853
Others say review them, analyze
them and detect attacks.

22
00:01:06.853 --> 00:01:11.214
An agency can satisfy the first
completely and still fail the rest.

23
00:01:11.214 --> 00:01:16.978
OMB's logging memo, M-26-14,
measures the same way.

24
00:01:16.978 --> 00:01:21.778
It sets retention floors, but its maturity
model scores collection by alerting,

25
00:01:21.778 --> 00:01:25.341
by how much of the baseline is
covered by actionable alerts.

26
00:01:25.341 --> 00:01:28.821
And here's the sharp example:
Microsoft says logs sent

27
00:01:28.821 --> 00:01:32.540
only to the Sentinel data lake
tier don't generate alerts.

28
00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.328
That source meets retention
and detects nothing.

29
00:01:35.328 --> 00:01:37.870
Next, why gaps stay silent.

30
00:01:38.620 --> 00:01:43.245
Coverage gaps don't announce themselves,
and there are four ways they stay quiet.

31
00:01:43.245 --> 00:01:46.887
Some sources are never connected;
Azure resource logs aren't

32
00:01:46.887 --> 00:01:50.383
collected until each resource
gets a diagnostic setting.

33
00:01:50.383 --> 00:01:52.822
Some connected sources aren't flowing;

34
00:01:52.822 --> 00:01:57.169
a green connector status can mean a
rule exists, not that data arrived.

35
00:01:57.169 --> 00:01:59.958
Some collected data is never analyzed.

36
00:01:59.958 --> 00:02:02.506
And some sources are
switched off on purpose,

37
00:02:02.506 --> 00:02:05.607
which CISA calls well-documented
attacker behavior.

38
00:02:05.607 --> 00:02:10.808
In the 2023 Exchange Online intrusion,
a federal victim caught it with its own

39
00:02:10.808 --> 00:02:15.683
alert on mailbox events that, at the
time, came only with a premium license.

40
00:02:15.683 --> 00:02:18.807
Agencies without those events
had nothing to look at.

41
00:02:18.807 --> 00:02:21.432
None of these gaps shows
up in what arrived,

42
00:02:21.432 --> 00:02:24.132
only in comparing it
with what was expected.

43
00:02:24.132 --> 00:02:26.782
That's why the inventory
is the denominator.

44
00:02:26.782 --> 00:02:29.250
Next, the evidence contract.

45
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.441
A multi-cloud agency runs
several stacks at once: Azure,

46
00:02:34.441 --> 00:02:39.375
AWS, a virtualization manager,
firewall syslog, a Linux agent.

47
00:02:39.375 --> 00:02:43.963
Five partial views can't prove that
one vulnerability is fixed everywhere.

48
00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:48.333
The program's answer is an evidence
contract: one record shape that

49
00:02:48.333 --> 00:02:52.624
every source emits, with eight
fields fixed before any data flows.

50
00:02:52.624 --> 00:02:56.121
They carry what NIST asks an
audit record to establish,

51
00:02:56.121 --> 00:02:59.850
plus the control it evidences,
a verdict and a disposition.

52
00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:03.625
The join key is authoritative
asset identity plus time.

53
00:03:03.625 --> 00:03:07.970
Every stack has its own identifier, so
the key comes from the naming plane,

54
00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.287
which has to exist before the pipeline.

55
00:03:10.287 --> 00:03:12.874
And time means when
the state was observed,

56
00:03:12.874 --> 00:03:17.087
because joining on arrival time
silently matches the wrong records.

57
00:03:17.087 --> 00:03:22.207
One caution: the system-wide, time-correlated
trail is a High-baseline control,

58
00:03:22.207 --> 00:03:25.326
so at Moderate the program
selects it by design.

59
00:03:25.326 --> 00:03:27.990
Next, what each plane must emit.

60
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:33.212
The program treats the estate
as six planes: identity,

61
00:03:33.212 --> 00:03:38.090
cloud platform, Microsoft 365,
endpoints, network and data.

62
00:03:38.090 --> 00:03:40.881
No single plane covers
the logging baseline,

63
00:03:40.881 --> 00:03:44.652
and systems a third party runs
for the agency are in scope too.

64
00:03:44.652 --> 00:03:48.316
Each plane has a default that
looks instrumented and isn't.

65
00:03:48.316 --> 00:03:51.928
Entra ID shows sign-ins,
but keeps them only 30 days,

66
00:03:51.928 --> 00:03:57.215
so the program streams every sign-in
stream, not just the interactive one.

67
00:03:57.215 --> 00:03:59.887
Azure drops its activity
log after 90 days,

68
00:03:59.887 --> 00:04:04.390
and resource logs aren't collected
at all without a diagnostic setting.

69
00:04:04.390 --> 00:04:08.185
Microsoft 365 audit
retention depends on license,

70
00:04:08.185 --> 00:04:12.252
and many users, guests included,
get the shorter window.

71
00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:14.622
So emission is enforced by policy,

72
00:04:14.622 --> 00:04:18.515
and verified by a query that
names every expected table.

73
00:04:18.515 --> 00:04:23.915
That way, a missing table shows up as a
row, instead of silently disappearing.

74
00:04:23.915 --> 00:04:27.260
Next, turning the
warehouse into detection.

75
00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:31.072
A workspace full of tables
is still a warehouse.

76
00:04:31.072 --> 00:04:35.785
Rules, incidents and automation are
what turn it into a detection engine.

77
00:04:35.785 --> 00:04:40.603
And the program's rule is strict: no source
counts as monitored until an enabled

78
00:04:40.603 --> 00:04:45.347
rule reads it, and no rule counts until
its output reaches a queue someone owns.

79
00:04:45.347 --> 00:04:48.747
The engine also sets a floor
on how fast you can detect.

80
00:04:48.747 --> 00:04:51.648
Scheduled rules run every
few minutes at best,

81
00:04:51.648 --> 00:04:55.007
near-real-time rules are
faster but limited in number,

82
00:04:55.007 --> 00:04:59.586
and behavioral baselines are slower
by design; the slide has the figures.

83
00:04:59.586 --> 00:05:05.523
Defender XDR detects what its own sensors
see on endpoints, identities and email.

84
00:05:05.523 --> 00:05:10.672
Sentinel rules cover what Defender
can't see, like DNS and other clouds.

85
00:05:10.672 --> 00:05:14.235
Then prepare for the assessor's
test: show a recent alert,

86
00:05:14.235 --> 00:05:18.185
the incident it opened, and what
was done, from alert to closure.

87
00:05:18.185 --> 00:05:20.510
Next, the federal clocks.

88
00:05:21.260 --> 00:05:23.710
Two federal clocks run against this work.

89
00:05:23.710 --> 00:05:30.423
The first is CISA's BOD
26-04, issued in June 2026.

90
00:05:30.423 --> 00:05:33.368
It replaced the older
vulnerability directives,

91
00:05:33.368 --> 00:05:38.037
and swapped severity buckets for four
questions about each vulnerable asset:

92
00:05:38.037 --> 00:05:41.341
is it publicly exposed, is the
flaw known to be exploited,

93
00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:45.148
can exploitation be automated,
and how much control does it give?

94
00:05:45.148 --> 00:05:49.718
The answers set a maximum timeline,
as short as three days, with forensic

95
00:05:49.718 --> 00:05:54.598
triage on the worst cases, because applying
a patch doesn't evict an attacker.

96
00:05:54.598 --> 00:05:58.580
The clock starts when CISA lists
the flaw or the agency finds it,

97
00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:01.510
whichever comes first,
and there are no waivers.

98
00:06:01.510 --> 00:06:05.185
Remediation within the table
applies from December 7.

99
00:06:05.185 --> 00:06:09.794
The second clock is M-26-14's:
a logging plan within 90 days of

100
00:06:09.794 --> 00:06:15.460
CISA's reference architecture, then
maturity levels on a set schedule.

101
00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:18.390
Next, vulnerability and patch.

102
00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:24.604
Vulnerability management fails at three
points assessors probe: a stale inventory,

103
00:06:24.604 --> 00:06:26.477
prioritizing by score alone,

104
00:06:26.477 --> 00:06:29.990
and no record of when something
was found, due and fixed.

105
00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:32.340
The program's answer is a daily queue.

106
00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:36.837
Pull the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities
feed and the scanner's findings,

107
00:06:36.837 --> 00:06:41.190
join each device to the authoritative
asset record and its exposure tag,

108
00:06:41.190 --> 00:06:45.977
apply the directive's table, and keep the
first-detected date, never resetting it.

109
00:06:45.977 --> 00:06:50.918
Two practical rules: treat an asset with
no exposure tag as publicly exposed,

110
00:06:50.918 --> 00:06:54.840
and keep your own history, because
the scanner's exports don't.

111
00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:59.577
The key idea is that a scanner authors
no state; it just measures a gap.

112
00:06:59.577 --> 00:07:02.740
Each platform patches with
its own native tools,

113
00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:07.564
but every one returns the same remediation
record, keyed to the same asset.

114
00:07:07.564 --> 00:07:12.203
A finding closes when the fix is recorded
and the next scan no longer sees it.

115
00:07:12.203 --> 00:07:14.590
Next, three more streams.

116
00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:17.753
Three more streams share the same queue.

117
00:07:17.753 --> 00:07:23.090
Configuration first: a patched host that
drifts from its baseline isn't remediated.

118
00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:27.095
And watch auto-correct features that
fix drift without recording it;

119
00:07:27.095 --> 00:07:29.765
the program keeps those
for low-risk settings.

120
00:07:29.765 --> 00:07:31.203
Cloud posture next.

121
00:07:31.203 --> 00:07:34.578
A storage account open to the
internet has no missing patch,

122
00:07:34.578 --> 00:07:37.127
so a vulnerability
scanner never reports it.

123
00:07:37.127 --> 00:07:41.001
Each provider's native posture tool
is primary for its own platform,

124
00:07:41.001 --> 00:07:44.390
and the notes cover where
cross-cloud coverage falls short.

125
00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:45.302
Then data.

126
00:07:45.302 --> 00:07:50.642
Controlled unclassified information
comes in two levels, Basic and Specified,

127
00:07:50.642 --> 00:07:55.902
decided by the CUI Registry, and labels
drive data loss prevention everywhere.

128
00:07:55.902 --> 00:07:58.552
Finally, check what
actually reaches the queue.

129
00:07:58.552 --> 00:08:02.863
Since October 2026, a built-in
Microsoft tuning rule keeps data loss

130
00:08:02.863 --> 00:08:07.403
signals out of the incident queue
unless an administrator turns it off.

131
00:08:07.403 --> 00:08:09.590
Next, who runs all this.

132
00:08:10.340 --> 00:08:12.405
Most agencies can name their SOC.

133
00:08:12.405 --> 00:08:16.994
Fewer can say who decided that a log
source is collected, how long it's kept,

134
00:08:16.994 --> 00:08:20.665
or who accepted the risk when
it went quiet for three weeks.

135
00:08:20.665 --> 00:08:23.078
The program assigns those decisions.

136
00:08:23.078 --> 00:08:25.414
The CIO sets scope and funding.

137
00:08:25.414 --> 00:08:29.697
The CISO decides what each plane
must emit, owns the logging plan,

138
00:08:29.697 --> 00:08:33.027
and approves any playbook
that acts on production.

139
00:08:33.027 --> 00:08:35.753
Platform owners keep
their planes emitting,

140
00:08:35.753 --> 00:08:39.878
security engineering builds the
tooling, and the SOC runs the queue.

141
00:08:39.878 --> 00:08:41.790
Two roles are easy to forget.

142
00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:45.794
The records officer must agree
before anyone shortens retention,

143
00:08:45.794 --> 00:08:48.415
because evidence that's
gone can't come back.

144
00:08:48.415 --> 00:08:52.543
And the authorizing official,
the only one who can accept risk,

145
00:08:52.543 --> 00:08:56.752
accepts the risk of not seeing:
a gap carried rather than closed,

146
00:08:56.752 --> 00:08:59.990
a suppression with no end
date, a missed deadline.

147
00:08:59.990 --> 00:09:02.600
Next, how detections get built.

148
00:09:03.350 --> 00:09:05.300
Detections are built as code.

149
00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:08.010
The rule library lives in version control,

150
00:09:08.010 --> 00:09:13.275
nobody edits a production rule in the portal,
and every change is a reviewed commit.

151
00:09:13.275 --> 00:09:17.350
Each rule has one named owner,
a test and a review date.

152
00:09:17.350 --> 00:09:21.153
Every rule moves through five
steps: author it, test it,

153
00:09:21.153 --> 00:09:25.475
run it alert-only, promote it,
and review it at least quarterly.

154
00:09:25.475 --> 00:09:27.062
The test matters most.

155
00:09:27.062 --> 00:09:28.693
Replay a known-bad sample,

156
00:09:28.693 --> 00:09:32.187
because a rule that can't be
shown to fire isn't finished.

157
00:09:32.187 --> 00:09:34.856
And run it alert-only
for at least two weeks,

158
00:09:34.856 --> 00:09:37.525
until its false positives are documented.

159
00:09:37.525 --> 00:09:42.104
Where a vendor analytic isn't available
in the agency's cloud, the program

160
00:09:42.104 --> 00:09:46.988
builds an equivalent inside its own
boundary, and says plainly it isn't parity.

161
00:09:46.988 --> 00:09:48.313
Then plan for rot.

162
00:09:48.313 --> 00:09:53.563
Thresholds drift, sources stop without an
error, and failing rules get disabled.

163
00:09:53.563 --> 00:09:57.175
That standing tuning effort is
the real cost of a library.

164
00:09:57.175 --> 00:09:59.060
Next, response.

165
00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:04.448
Detection without response shortens
the investigation, not the exposure.

166
00:10:04.448 --> 00:10:09.820
So every playbook is gated by confidence:
high confidence prepares containment,

167
00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:13.669
medium confidence enriches and
opens a ticket for a human,

168
00:10:13.669 --> 00:10:15.673
and low confidence never acts.

169
00:10:15.673 --> 00:10:20.570
Responses sit on the workbook's actuation
ladder, from observing and advising,

170
00:10:20.570 --> 00:10:23.735
up to gated actuation,
where the playbook prepares

171
00:10:23.735 --> 00:10:26.372
an action and a named
approver releases it.

172
00:10:26.372 --> 00:10:29.935
That's the default for anything
with a large blast radius.

173
00:10:29.935 --> 00:10:34.785
Only listed, low-risk classes run on
their own, logged and reversible.

174
00:10:34.785 --> 00:10:39.005
A few, like isolating a server or
disabling a privileged account,

175
00:10:39.005 --> 00:10:40.597
always need an approver.

176
00:10:40.597 --> 00:10:43.912
And because Defender's attack
disruption acts on its own,

177
00:10:43.912 --> 00:10:47.297
the authorizing official
decides whether to accept that.

178
00:10:47.297 --> 00:10:49.098
Latency is real too.

179
00:10:49.098 --> 00:10:52.914
A hybrid user must be disabled
in Active Directory as well,

180
00:10:52.914 --> 00:10:54.584
or the next sync undoes it.

181
00:10:54.584 --> 00:10:56.800
Next, proving it works.

182
00:10:57.550 --> 00:10:59.062
Last, prove it works.

183
00:10:59.062 --> 00:11:03.323
Continuous monitoring is a
pipeline: emit, normalize,

184
00:11:03.323 --> 00:11:08.262
correlate, bind each record to
its control, report, and respond.

185
00:11:08.262 --> 00:11:10.687
The metrics are built
so they can't flatter.

186
00:11:10.687 --> 00:11:15.463
Detection coverage counts how many
baseline activities have an enabled,

187
00:11:15.463 --> 00:11:19.063
tested rule over a fresh source,
not how many rules exist.

188
00:11:19.063 --> 00:11:21.924
A stale required source
counts as a failure.

189
00:11:21.924 --> 00:11:26.044
Vulnerability metrics count closures
on time plus items past due,

190
00:11:26.044 --> 00:11:30.987
because a percentage of the open queue
improves every time new findings arrive.

191
00:11:30.987 --> 00:11:35.649
And configuration reports passing,
failing and excepted settings separately.

192
00:11:35.649 --> 00:11:37.788
Read outside scores carefully, too.

193
00:11:37.788 --> 00:11:41.340
The logging memo scores a
system at its weakest element,

194
00:11:41.340 --> 00:11:46.050
and FedRAMP's Key Security Indicators
bind cloud providers, not agencies.

195
00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:49.120
In the end, a finding
closes on re-test evidence,

196
00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:52.837
or the authorizing official
carries it with an end date.

197
00:11:52.837 --> 00:11:55.040
Let's close with the decisions.

198
00:11:55.790 --> 00:11:57.165
So, four decisions.

199
00:11:57.165 --> 00:12:00.224
Name the planes and assets
that are expected to emit,

200
00:12:00.224 --> 00:12:03.140
and check each one against
what actually arrives.

201
00:12:03.140 --> 00:12:06.548
Stand up the naming plane
before the evidence pipeline,

202
00:12:06.548 --> 00:12:09.215
so every record joins
on one asset identity.

203
00:12:09.215 --> 00:12:14.465
Fund the logging plan as the program's
visibility plan, not as a storage purchase.

204
00:12:14.465 --> 00:12:19.592
And send every blind spot to the authorizing
official, whether it's a telemetry gap,

205
00:12:19.592 --> 00:12:23.815
an open-ended suppression or a missed
deadline, each with an end date.

206
00:12:23.815 --> 00:12:26.015
The next step needs no procurement.

207
00:12:26.015 --> 00:12:28.923
Run the coverage check
against the inventory,

208
00:12:28.923 --> 00:12:33.514
tag the exposure of every externally
reachable asset before December 7,

209
00:12:33.514 --> 00:12:37.340
and count a source as covered
only when a tested rule reads it.

210
00:12:37.340 --> 00:12:39.503
You can't defend what you can't see,

211
00:12:39.503 --> 00:12:43.428
and a finding closes only when
the thing is fixed, with proof.

212
00:12:43.428 --> 00:12:47.116
Book 12 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

213
00:12:47.116 --> 00:12:47.940
Thank you.
